The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}
```

Then your production code depends on `IFileSystem` (injected via constructor), and tests supply a fake/mock implementation.

**Alternatives:**

1. **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box, plus an in-memory implementation (`MockFileSystem`) for tests.
2. **`Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders`** — lighter-weight, read-focused abstraction already in the framework.

**Recommendation:** Use `System.IO.Abstractions` if you want minimal boilerplate:

```csharp
// Production
public class MyService(IFileSystem fs)
{
    public void Save(string path, string data) => fs.File.WriteAllText(path, data);
}

// Test
var fs = new MockFileSystem();
var svc = new MyService(fs);
svc.Save("/tmp/test.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", fs.File.ReadAllText("/tmp/test.txt"));
```

This gives you full testability without touching the real disk.
